{"command": "cd /testbed && head -60 specs/001-transactions/spec.md", "exit_code": 0, "stderr": "", "stdout": "# Feature Specification: Batch Journal Writer for TinyDB\n\n**Feature Branch**: `001-transactions`\n\n**Created**: 2026-10-17\n\n**Status**: Draft\n\n**Input**: User description: \"Build tinydb.journal.BatchWriter(db), wrapping a supplied TinyDB instance without changing its ordinary APIs. Use the default table only. R01: apply(operations) accepts a list of operation dictionaries (insert document / remove by doc_id), returns inserted IDs, [] for empty input, ValueError for unknown/malformed operations. R02: batch application is atomic with full rollback of the pre-batch default-table data on any error; missing-ID remove is an error; previously acquired default-table handles must reflect success and rollback. R03: ordinary TinyDB reads/writes and preexisting documents continue to work; inserted and returned nested data must not alias the caller's input.\"\n\n## User Scenarios & Testing *(mandatory)*\n\n### User Story 1 - Apply a Batch of Inserts and Removes (Priority: P1)\n\nAn application developer wraps an existing TinyDB instance with a batch writer and submits a list of operations (insert a document, or remove a document by ID) in a single call. The call returns the IDs of the documents it inserted; removes contribute no result. This is the core value: a multi-step change expressed and applied as one batch.\n\n**Why this priority**: Without successful batch application there is no feature; every other story builds on it.\n\n**Independent Test**: On an empty database, apply one insert and verify the returned ID and that the document is visible through the ordinary read API; then apply one remove of that ID and verify the table is empty and the result is [].\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** an empty default table, **When** the batch is applied with one insert of a document, **Then** the call returns the single inserted ID and the document is readable through the ordinary TinyDB read APIs.\n2. **Given** a default table containing the document with the given ID, **When** the batch is applied with one remove for that ID, **Then** the call returns [] and the table no longer contains the document.\n3. **Given** any default-table state, **When** the batch is applied with an empty operation list, **Then** the call returns [] and the table is unchanged.\n4. **Given** a batch mixing inserts and removes, **When** it is applied successfully, **Then** operations take effect in list order and the result is exactly the inserted IDs in insertion order.\n\n---\n\n### User Story 2 - Atomic Batch with Full Rollback (Priority: P1)\n\nA developer relies on the batch being all-or-nothing: if any operation in the batch fails, the default table is restored to exactly its pre-batch state, and the failure is reported as an error. Handles to the default table that were acquired before the batch call must observe the outcome without needing to be re-acquired.\n\n**Why this priority**: A partially applied batch would silently corrupt data; atomicity is a load-bearing guarantee, not a nice-to-have.\n\n**Independent Test**: Seed one preexisting document, then apply a batch whose second operation removes a missing ID; verify the error is raised and the table contains exactly the preexisting document.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** pre-batch default-table data and a batch in which a later operation fails (for example, a remove targeting a missing ID), **When** the batch is applied, **Then** an error is raised and the default table is restored to exactly its complete pre-batch data (documents, IDs, and order).\n2. **Given** a previously acquired handle to the default table, **When** a batch succeeds or rolls back, **Then** that handle reflects the post-batch state on success and the restored pre-batch state on rollback, without re-acquisition.\n3. **Given** a batch whose first operation fails, **When** it is applied, **Then** no partial changes remain in the default table.\n4. **Given** a batch that removes an ID inserted by an earlier operation in the same batch, **When** it is applied, **Then** the remove is valid (sequential semantics) and the batch succeeds.\n\n---\n\n### User Story 3 - Strict Validation and Safe Data Handling (Priority: P2)\n\nA developer expects malformed input to be rejected loudly and predictably, and expects no hidden sharing between their in-memory data and the database: mutating their documents or operations after a successful apply must not alter stored data, and ordinary TinyDB usage is unaffected by the wrapper.\n\n**Why this priority**: Validation and aliasing safety protect data integrity at the edges of the feature; they do not block the core flow but are required for trustworthy use.\n\n**Independent Test**: Apply a batch containing an unknown operation type and verify ValueError is raised with the table unchanged; then apply a valid batch with a nested document, mutate the caller's copy afterward, and verify the stored document is unchanged.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** an operation with an unknown or missing op type, **When** the batch is applied, **Then** ValueError is raised and the default table is unchanged.\n2. **Given** a malformed operation (missing 'document' on an insert, a non-dict document, a missing/non-integer/non-positive 'doc_id' on a remove, or a non-dict operation entry), **When** the batch is applied, **Then** ValueError is raised and the default table is unchanged.\n3. **Given** a successful batch that inserted a nested document, **When** the caller mutates the original document or operation afterward, **Then** the stored data is unchanged and the returned data is not the same object as the caller's input.\n4. **Given** a wrapped database with preexisting documents, **When** ordinary TinyDB reads and writes are used alongside the writer, **Then** they continue to work and observe the same data as the writer.\n"}